                <p class="snippet" >
                    Wilson v. The Queen, [1983] 2 S.C.R. 594; <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc., [1984] 2 S.C.R. 145; Slaight Communications Inc. v. Davidson, [1989] 1 S.C.R. 1038. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;This and the fact that the section is not attacked is conclusive against the contention that the "resort to" clause is, per se, contrary to the requirements of s. 8 of the Charter as laid down in <b>Hunter v. Southam</b>, supra. <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> concerned a search warrant, whereas this case concerns electronic interception. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;In <b>Hunter v. Southam</b>, this Court enunciated the standards against which the courts are to assess the reasonableness of a given search and seizure. 
