<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/1097/index.do?q=%22Hunter+v.+Southam%22">R. v. Colarusso</a></span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[1994] 1 SCR 20</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">1994-01-26</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Constitutional law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Given this irrelevancy, the applicability of the other <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc. criteria was doubtful. No police seizure occurred here. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;The <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc. criterion that there be reasonable and probable cause to believe that a specific offence has been committed is inapplicable in this context. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc., [1984] 2 S.C.R. 145, teaches us that s. 8, like other Charter rights, must be broadly and liberally construed to effect its purpose. 
                </p>
