    <li class="odd list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">7.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/4628/index.do?q=%22Hunter+v.+Southam%22">R. v. A.M.</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2008 SCC 19</span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[2008] 1 SCR 569</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2008-04-25</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Constitutional law<br/>
                            Professional law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    For reasons to be explained, I would not go so far as the accused who insists that the full <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc., [1984] 2 S.C.R. 145, requirement of prior judicial authorization must be imposed. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[10] In the United States, from whose Fourth Amendment decisions <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> drew inspiration, a series of divided Supreme Court decisions has declined to grant any Fourth Amendment protection against “narcotic” sniffer dogs. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;The <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> requirement of prior judicial authorization is the gold standard because an important purpose of s. 8 is to prevent unreasonable searches and not in the usual case just to give an after-the-fact remedy. 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
