                    See Attorney General of Nova Scotia v. MacIntyre, supra; <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc., [1984] 2 S.C.R. 145, at pp. 159-60; R. v. Amway Corp., [1989] 1 S.C.R. 21, at p. 40, and R. v. Dyment, [1988] 2 S.C.R. 417, at pp. 427-28. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;This Court has recently considered the right to privacy in cases involving the search of a person's property without his or her consent (see <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc., [1984] 2 S.C.R. 145; R. v. Genest, [1989] 1 S.C.R. 59) and the search of a person's body without his or her consent (see R. v. Beare, [1988] 2 S.C.R. 387; R. v.&nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;In <b>Hunter v. Southam</b> Inc., [1984] 2 S.C.R. 145, it was held to underlie the protection against unreasonable search and seizure enshrined in s. 8 of the Charter . 
